<h1>{{ title }}</h1>

<p>On October 19 and 20, 2026, we linked audit records that we identified as resubmissions of one another. This page lists every affected record from these runs.</p>

<h2>What we did</h2>

<p>Some audits were submitted more than once for the same auditee and audit year. This is because resubmission was not yet feature-complete, and entities needed to issue up-to-date corrections regardless of our status.</p>
<p>For these groups of records, the "original" or "first version" was intended to be made obsolete, or otherwise marked as incorrect. We have taken a first pass at finding these groups of records and linking them such that the incorrect "original" is marked DEPRECATED, and is no longer publicly available.</p>

<h2>Note</h2>

<p>We have taken care that our first pass only identifies records we can say with certainty are resubmissions of one another. To meet our process, these records must have duplicate:</p>
<ul>
    <li><code>audit_year</code></li>
    <li><code>auditee_uei</code></li>
    <li><code>auditee_ein</code></li>
    <li><code>auditee_email</code></li>
    <li><code>auditee_name</code></li>
    <li><code>auditee_state</code></li>
</ul>
<p>If any of these values were updated by the resubmission, they were not identified with this pass.</p>
<p>The list of affected records is quite long (3,515 records), and may be difficult to digest in web format. For a single searchable and machine-readable format,
    <a href="{{ config.baseUrl }}assets/curation/resubmission-chains-rollup.csv" download>Download the full list of linked records (CSV)</a>, covering all audit years.
    All records in the list were affected by having metadata added to them. Only the first value(s) in a chain were marked "DEPRECATED".
</p>

